{
  "id": "Compound::DB00492 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Fosinopril, a widely used ACE inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of hypertension through multiple interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Fosinopril causes Oligohydramnios or Skull Hypoplasia, both of which are linked to Captopril, an ACE inhibitor with similar pharmacological properties. Captopril, in turn, is known to treat hypertension, indicating a direct therapeutic relationship between Captopril and the disease. This suggests that Fosinopril may exert a causal influence on hypertension through its role in inducing conditions that are associated with the disease. The shared ancestry of Fosinopril and Captopril within the ACE inhibitor class further supports a mechanistic link, highlighting the potential for Fosinopril to contribute to hypertension through its pharmacological effects. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of considering the broader pharmacological context when evaluating the reutilization of Fosinopril for hypertension.",
